    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dubailand was conceived as a massive entertainment complex, with Universal Studios announced in 2007, aiming to draw 5 million visitors annually.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Studios_Dubailand"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Construction stalled after 2009, and by 2012 only a Universal Studios logo gate remained visible.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Studios_Dubailand"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 2016, Dubai officials officially cancelled the Universal Studios Dubailand project, ending the decade‑long plan.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Studios_Dubailand"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The original plan included themed areas like a Hollywood‑style district, a Mel’s Diner replica, and a Ghostbusters trackless ride.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Studios_Dubailand"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Dubailand’s development was tied to Tatweer, a Dubai Holdings subsidiary, which dissolved in 2010, leaving the joint venture unclear.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Studios_Dubailand"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The area was intended to sit just outside central Dubai, near the world’s largest mall and a Universal Studios branded family entertainment centre.",
